from (values
('113.46.c.15.B',3,'A current issue in American education is what?','["Equal funding everywhere", "Universal agreement on curriculum", "No standardized tests", "Funding gaps between wealthy and poor school districts"]'::jsonb,3,'Debates include testing and school choice.','original'),
('113.46.c.15.B',3,'School choice programs allow families to do what?','["Select schools outside their assigned district, sometimes with public funds", "Hire teachers directly", "Avoid all schooling", "Pay no taxes"]'::jsonb,0,'Supporters and critics disagree.','original'),
('113.46.c.15.C',3,'From a sociological viewpoint, religion is studied as what?','["Only a private feeling", "A social institution that shapes beliefs and behavior", "A scientific theory", "A form of government"]'::jsonb,1,'Sociologists do not judge whether beliefs are true.','original'),
('113.46.c.15.C',3,'Durkheim argued that religion does what?','["Has no social effects", "Is purely individual", "Strengthens social solidarity through shared rituals", "Divides every group"]'::jsonb,2,'He saw sacred symbols as expressing society.','original'),
('113.46.c.15.D',3,'Religion serves a basic societal need by doing what?','["Printing money", "Setting tax rates", "Enforcing traffic laws", "Providing meaning, community and moral guidance"]'::jsonb,3,'It supports social cohesion.','original'),
('113.46.c.15.D',3,'A rite of passage in religion marks what?','["A transition in a person''s life such as a baptism or bar mitzvah", "A tax filing", "A sporting event", "A trade agreement"]'::jsonb,0,'These rituals mark status changes.','original'),
('113.46.c.15.E',3,'A feature of religion in the United States is what?','["A single state religion", "Religious freedom protected by the First Amendment and great diversity", "Government control of churches", "A ban on religion"]'::jsonb,1,'Many other nations have established religions.','original'),
('113.46.c.15.E',3,'In some countries, an established or state religion means what?','["Religions are equal", "Total religious freedom", "The government officially supports one faith", "No religion is allowed"]'::jsonb,2,'The U.S. separates church and state.','original'),
('113.46.c.16.A',3,'Science became institutionalized as a result of what?','["Folk tradition", "Religious ritual", "Military draft", "Universities, funding systems and the scientific method"]'::jsonb,3,'Organized research expanded.','original'),
('113.46.c.16.A',3,'One social effect of scientific institutions is what?','["Technological change that alters work and daily life", "Loss of education", "Fewer discoveries", "No change"]'::jsonb,0,'Science drives social change.','original'),
('113.46.c.16.B',3,'Which was the earliest form of mass media?','["Radio", "Print such as books and newspapers", "The internet", "Television"]'::jsonb,1,'Printing technology came first.','original'),
('113.46.c.16.B',3,'The internet differs from earlier mass media because it allows what?','["No audience", "Only one-way messages", "Interactive, two-way communication among users", "Only print news"]'::jsonb,2,'Social media is user-generated.','original'),
('113.46.c.16.C',3,'Functionalists see mass media as serving which role?','["Causing deviance only", "Hiding information", "Exploiting audiences only", "Informing, entertaining and transmitting culture"]'::jsonb,3,'Media supports social integration.','original'),
('113.46.c.16.C',3,'Conflict theorists view mass media as what?','["A tool used by powerful groups to promote their interests", "Always beneficial to all", "A neutral mirror", "A tool of the poor"]'::jsonb,0,'Ownership concentration is a concern.','original'),
('113.46.c.16.D',3,'A contemporary issue in mass media is what?','["Lack of choices", "Misinformation spreading rapidly online", "Slow communication", "Absence of media"]'::jsonb,1,'Media literacy helps.','original'),
('113.46.c.16.D',3,'Media concentration means that what is true?','["No ownership", "Many small owners", "A few corporations own many media outlets", "The government owns none"]'::jsonb,2,'It can limit diversity of viewpoints.','original'),
('113.46.c.17.A',3,'Demography is the study of what?','["Languages only", "Weather", "Rocks", "Population size, composition and change"]'::jsonb,3,'It uses births, deaths and migration.','original'),
('113.46.c.17.A',3,'The birth rate is measured as what?','["The number of births per 1,000 people in a year", "The number of migrants per 100", "The number of deaths per 1,000", "The number of marriages per 1,000"]'::jsonb,0,'It is a basic demographic measure.','original'),
('113.46.c.17.B',3,'Thomas Malthus argued that what would happen?','["Food supply outpaces population", "Population grows faster than food supply, leading to famine and war", "Population always shrinks", "Technology ends hunger"]'::jsonb,1,'His theory was criticized as too pessimistic.','original'),
('113.46.c.17.B',3,'The demographic transition theory describes what?','["Rapid population decline", "Constant birth rates", "A shift from high birth and death rates to low ones as societies industrialize", "Migration only"]'::jsonb,2,'Population growth slows in later stages.','original'),
('113.46.c.18.A',3,'A social movement is best described as what?','["A random event", "A spontaneous crowd", "A fad", "An organized, long-term effort to promote or resist social change"]'::jsonb,3,'The civil rights movement is an example.','original'),
('113.46.c.18.A',3,'A fad differs from a social movement because a fad is what?','["Short-lived and focused on style rather than change", "Organized by law", "Political", "Permanent"]'::jsonb,0,'Fads fade quickly.','original'),
('113.46.c.18.B',3,'Relative deprivation theory says that social movements arise when what?','["People have equal wealth", "People feel they have less than they deserve compared with others", "Government is absent", "Everyone is satisfied"]'::jsonb,1,'Perceived gaps trigger action.','original'),
('113.46.c.18.B',3,'Resource mobilization theory emphasizes that movements need what?','["Only media", "Only anger", "Money, organization and leadership to succeed", "Only luck"]'::jsonb,2,'Resources determine success.','original'),
('113.46.c.18.C',3,'Which social process contributes to social change?','["Isolation", "Stagnation", "Ritual only", "Innovation, such as new technology"]'::jsonb,3,'Discovery and diffusion also contribute.','original'),
('113.46.c.18.C',3,'Diffusion as a source of social change means what?','["The spread of cultural traits from one society to another", "Invention of new ideas", "Hidden discovery", "Loss of culture"]'::jsonb,0,'Trade and media spread culture.','original'),
('113.46.c.19.A',3,'A strong sociological product on a current issue relies on what?','["One anecdote", "Credible sources and critical analysis", "Personal bias only", "Rumor"]'::jsonb,1,'Evidence supports conclusions.','original'),
('113.46.c.19.A',3,'In a survey, a random sample is used to do what?','["Avoid data", "Prove a belief", "Represent the larger population fairly", "Include only friends"]'::jsonb,2,'Random selection reduces bias.','original'),
('113.46.c.19.B',3,'In a cause-and-effect analysis, a correlation does not necessarily show what?','["That a pattern exists", "That variables are related", "That data exist", "That one variable causes the other"]'::jsonb,3,'Other factors may be involved.','original'),
('113.46.c.19.B',3,'Categorizing survey responses into groups is an example of what skill?','["Organizing data", "Altering data", "Ignoring data", "Hiding data"]'::jsonb,0,'It helps find patterns.','original'),
('113.46.c.19.C',3,'A percentage helps interpret sociological data by doing what?','["Replacing data", "Allowing comparison of groups of different sizes", "Showing only totals", "Hiding differences"]'::jsonb,1,'Rates standardize comparisons.','original'),
('113.46.c.19.C',3,'The median of a data set is what?','["The most common value", "The sum of values", "The middle value when numbers are ordered", "The highest value"]'::jsonb,2,'It is less affected by extreme incomes.','original'),
('113.46.c.20.A',3,'Using the word "norm" correctly in sociology means what?','["A mathematical average only", "A crime", "A building", "An expected standard of behavior"]'::jsonb,3,'Precise terms avoid confusion.','original')
